# colonnade — the forecourt colonnade: two arms of Tuscan columns four deep round an oval court.
# A FRAGMENT of clips/facility.clip. Its twin, clips/estate/colonnade.clip, is the standalone clip
# this was converted from and is unchanged; the two are copies, not a move.
#
# SIX whole-clip statements are deleted here, because the manifest owns all of them: the
# `_contract.clip` include (a second copy redefines every param and every material), `metre 16`,
# `bounds`, `variation`, `origin` and `solid part_colonnade`.
#
# `metre 16` IS THE ONE THAT DIFFERS FROM THE OTHER SIX FRAGMENTS. This building was authored at
# sixteen voxels a metre and the manifest samples at thirty-two. That is safe in this direction and
# only this one: every feature that resolves at 1/16 m also resolves at 1/32, and this file's
# smallest members were sized against the coarser voxel, so none can fall through. A fragment
# authored FINER than the manifest would be the dangerous way round.
#
# WHERE IT STANDS — (0, 0, -29.00). Drawn with the centre of the oval at the origin and the facility
# to the north (+z); the oval's centre lands at facility z = -29.00, the arms open northward toward
# the great steps (which end at z = -15.70, x +-9.45), and the gate stands across z ~ -43.
#
# IT IS THE TIGHTEST FIT ON THE ESTATE. Translated it reaches z = -45.65 against the manifest's
# `bounds` low of -46: THIRTY-FIVE CENTIMETRES, eleven voxels at metre 32. Its own weathering is
# 0.16 and 0.14, so it holds -- but matter outside the bounds is not clipped, it is simply never
# sampled, so anything added at the gate end comes back with a flat face and no error.
#
# EVERY SHAPE A RULE NAMES HAS A TRANSLATED TWIN, `<name>_at`, standing directly after the shape it
# carries. A translate moves the stone and leaves every binding where it was drawn, so a rule keyed
# on an untranslated shape paints a colonnade-shaped volume of air 29 m north of the colonnade --
# and the material list still comes out with every coat at nearly the right share, because the paint
# did land on something. `on=` is a shape like any other and takes the twin too. `below=` goes 0.02
# to 0.035 with the translate, per clips/estate/BRIEF.md rule 5.
#
#
# Bernini's embrace at the scale of a country palace. Two arms sweep out of the flanks of the
# facility's great steps, close round an oval forecourt, and end in a gate; a hundred and
# sixty-eight free-standing columns stand on twenty-one radial rays, four to a ray, carrying a
# plain entablature and a balustrade with statues and urns on it.
#
# THIS IS A STANDALONE CLIP. It is not a fragment of clips/facility.clip and must not be added to
# it. Open it on its own:
#
#     build\bin\WorldShaper.exe --clip-file clips\estate\colonnade.clip
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHERE IT STANDS ON THE ESTATE
#
# Built in its own coordinates, with the centre of the oval at the origin, the facility to the
# NORTH (+z) and the gate to the SOUTH (-z) — the same north as the estate's. To place it in the
# facility's frame, add (0, 0, -29.00): the oval's centre is at facility z = -29.00, the arms open
# northward toward the great steps (which end at facility z = -15.70, x +-9.45), and the gate
# stands across facility z ~ -43. The site plan in clips/estate/BRIEF.md gives this building
# "z -16 .. -40", 24 m of depth. AN OVAL 48.6 m WIDE CANNOT BE 24 m DEEP AND STILL BE AN OVAL, and
# the brief's own 48 x 34 does not fit that row either. This one is 48.60 x 28.80 m on the outer
# row — wider and shallower than the brief's suggestion, which is what makes it an EMBRACE of the
# steps rather than a ring in front of them — and it reaches about three metres further south than
# the table says. The triumphal gate at facility z ~ -44 stands just beyond it.
#
# The arms' north ends stop at x = +-12.39, which is 2.94 m clear of the great steps at +-9.45.
#
# ONE MORE SHIFT, AT THE VERY END OF THE FILE. `origin 0 -1.60 -7.20` moves the finished clip so
# that a player spawning at 0,0,0 stands on the paving 7.20 m (16 M) north of the obelisk rather
# than inside its plinth, which is what the middle of this clip is. So every number ABOVE is the
# design frame, and the game's world coordinates are those less (0, 1.60, 7.20). Put the two
# together and facility coordinates are world + (0, 1.60, -21.80).
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE METRE, AND WHAT IT COSTS. AUTHORED AT metre 16, DELIBERATELY.
#
# A clip is a dense array over its whole bounds. This box is 53.10 x 18.45 x 31.50 m. At the
# contract's metre 32 that is 1.01 THOUSAND MILLION cells — five gigabytes at the five bytes a
# cell the game uses and rather more in the measuring tool — before a voxel of stone exists. At
# metre 16 it is 127 million cells, which IS a scene. MEASURED, on four cores that thirteen other
# builds were using at the same time:
#
#   metre  8    15.9 M cells    1.89 M voxels of matter    222 MB     63 s
#   metre 16     127 M cells    15.1 M voxels of matter   1.74 GB    565 s
#
# Eight times the cells for nine times the time, so nothing here is quadratic — but 1.74 GB for
# 127 M cells is THIRTEEN AND A HALF BYTES A CELL against the five the game's own array uses, and
# metre 32 on this box would be a thousand million cells, two hours and fourteen gigabytes. That
# is the whole argument for the estate being a family of clips rather than one, restated with this
# building's own numbers.
#
# AND THE COMPONENT COUNT IS NOT ONE, AND THAT IS REPORTED RATHER THAN HIDDEN. At metre 16 it is
# 465 components with 646 floating voxels out of 15,136,537 — four thousandths of one per cent —
# and every floater is four or eight voxels of the matter this clip exists to put at the edge of
# resolvable: a gilt spearhead 0.11 m across, a lip of the fountain coping, the raised hand of a
# statue. THE STONE IS ONE PIECE: the largest component is 15,135,891 voxels, which is everything
# but those specks. Four real breaks were found this way and fixed — 126 columns standing on
# nothing (the frame sign, below), the fountain's upper bowl on a 0.060 m baluster neck, the gate's
# crown over the hole up the middle of its own revolve, and the coping struck from the basin's own
# radius so that it met it along a line rather than through a volume. If this number climbs back
# into the thousands, one of those four has come back.
#
# WHAT metre 16 COSTS: a voxel is 0.0625 m. The gate's bars are 0.05 m, so they are SUB-VOXEL and
# will come out ragged, broken or gone. THAT IS THE POINT AND THEY ARE NOT TO BE THICKENED — see
# the fourth load below. The baluster necks (0.060) are the same case. Everything else in this
# building is a large outdoor structure with no one-voxel feature in it: the smallest members that
# matter are the guttae (0.060), the balusters (0.180 at the plinth) and the mutules (0.135 deep),
# and the columns are 0.90 across. metre 16 is the right resolution for it and metre 32 is not
# affordable for it. Checked at BOTH 8 and 16.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS PART PUTS UNDER LOAD — read this before touching a number.
#
# 1. THE LARGEST REPETITION IN THE PROJECT, IN DEPTH. A hundred and sixty-eight columns on
#    twenty-one radial rays means that from almost anywhere on the ground you are looking DOWN a
#    colonnade: a near shaft a metre across, a far shaft a few pixels across, and every size in
#    between, in one frame, on one object. That is the continuous-detail promise stated as a
#    picture — detail as a continuous function of how many pixels a thing covers, with no
#    level-of-detail steps anywhere — and it is the scene where a step in that function cannot be
#    hidden, because the eye walks along the row and any discontinuity travels with it. The rows
#    are RADIAL: a column in row 1 lines up with its fellows in rows 2, 3 and 4 along a ray from
#    the centre of the oval, so the alignment is exact from the middle of the court and shears
#    away as you walk. Do not make the count smaller. The count is the load.
#
# 2. DEEP SHADOW WITH A BRIGHT FLOOR IN IT. The colonnade is ROOFED: 1.80 m of entablature over
#    6.30 m of depth, from 6.84 to 8.64, open at both ends, standing over pale travertine paving in
#    full sun. Every surface inside it — the north face of every shaft, the whole soffit, the
#    hundred and fifty-six sunken coffers in it — is lit almost entirely by light that has
#    bounced off the floor, and the bands of sun between the four rows are the brightest thing in
#    the scene. It is the portico's problem twenty times over and out of doors.
#
# 3. IRREGULAR MASSES ON A SKYLINE. Forty-two statues 2.70 m tall and forty urns stand on the
#    balustrade, plus two more statues on the gate piers: EIGHTY-FOUR separate irregular shapes
#    against the sky, in four figure types turned to face the court, at a distance where each is a
#    handful of pixels. Nothing else on the estate produces that. They are coarse on purpose —
#    they are not sculpture, they are silhouettes.
#
# 4. THIN IRON AGAINST BRIGHT SKY. The gate's bars are 0.05 m and its scrollwork 0.05 m: ONE TO
#    TWO VOXELS at metre 32 and SUB-VOXEL at metre 16, standing in an open gateway with nothing
#    but sky behind them. THE GATE IS DELIBERATELY AT THE EDGE OF RESOLVABLE AND IS THERE TO FIND
#    OUT WHAT HAPPENS TO IT. If it comes out dotted, striped or missing at metre 16, that is a
#    measurement of the sampler, not a fault in the gate, and thickening it deletes the
#    measurement.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ORDER IS TUSCAN, AND IT IS NOT THE FACILITY'S.
#
# The facility is Ionic — 9 diameters, an Attic base, twenty-four flutes, volutes. A forecourt
# colonnade in the same order competes with the building it serves, so this one is TUSCAN: 7
# diameters, a plinth-and-torus base, a plain unfluted shaft, a cushion capital of an astragal,
# an ovolo echinus and a square abacus. That is the correct classical answer — the outer order is
# always the plainer one — and it is also a completely different set of shapes for the sampler:
# no spiral, no polar fold, no 0.030 m fillet, and a very large number of surfaces of revolution.
#
# THE MODULE IS THE FACILITY'S, so the two buildings are the same size of thing:
#
#   D = 0.90   the column at its foot        M = 0.45   the unit everything is counted in
#   column     7 D = 6.30   base 0.5 D, shaft 6 D, capital 0.5 D
#   entabl.    D/4 x column = 1.575, taken as 1.80 (4 M)
#   balustrade 2.4 M = 1.08
#   bay        4.5 M = 2.025, measured as ARC along the outer row, so it is the same everywhere
#   rows       4 M = 1.80 apart, radially: 0.90 m of daylight between two rows
#   steps      0.18 rise, 0.32 run, without exception
#
# Three numbers in this file are constructions rather than dimensions, and each is named where it
# occurs: the entasis arc (centre -85.973, 1.5002; radius 86.436) whose only job is to pass
# through r(0) = 0.450 = M, r(5.40) = 0.375 = 5M/6 and a crown of 0.4625 a third of the way up;
# the drain dish (a sphere of radius 1296) whose only job is to fall 0.18 = 0.4 M over the 21.60 m
# of the court; and the ellipse itself, whose semi-axes 24.30 = 54 M and 14.40 = 32 M are module
# numbers but whose polar radius between them is whatever the ellipse says it is.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW IT IS BUILT, AND THE TWO THINGS THAT WOULD HAVE MADE IT UNOPENABLE
#
# The arms are drawn ONCE, on +x, and folded with `mirror axis=x`. Everything on them is one unit
# drawn once and carried to twenty-six places by a rotation and a translation, which are
# isometries and therefore exact and boxed.
#
#   NO `around`. A polar fold reports a distance larger than the truth near a sector boundary, so
#   the field declares it infinitely slack, and infinite slack anywhere means the sampler can skip
#   nothing ANYWHERE. _order.clip measured that at 85 million field calls for one column.
#
#   NO NON-UNIFORM `scale`. field.cpp gives a non-uniformly scaled node the box `everywhere()` —
#   on purpose, because it under-reports distance and a cull reading its box would drop it — and
#   an unbounded child makes its parent unbounded all the way up. An elliptical stylobate written
#   as a scaled cylinder would have taken the box off this entire clip. So the ring is built the
#   way a real one is: STRAIGHT SEGMENTS BETWEEN ADJACENT COLUMNS, twenty-six of them per arm,
#   each overlapping its neighbours, with the mouldings run straight. Nothing here bends a
#   moulding. The outer face is a true polygon and the joints are meant to be seen.
#
# The units are all the same size — 2.40 m of run, 1.08 m out from the chord and 6.30 m in — and
# the generator that laid them out checks, for every one of the 84 column axes on an arm, that it
# stands on a stylobate block and under an entablature block with its full 0.63 m plinth inside.
# Adjacent blocks are 5-6 degrees apart, so at depth they overlap: there is no gap to fall through
# and the inner edge undulates by 0.04 m, which is under a voxel.
#
# WHY THE ROWS SHEAR. The rows are offset along RAYS FROM THE CENTRE, and a ray of an ellipse is
# not perpendicular to it except at the two apses — at the arm ends the ray is 28 degrees off the
# normal. So the inner rows slide sideways relative to the outer one as you go out along the arm.
# That is not an error; it is what a radial layout on an oval does, it is what Bernini's does, and
# it is the whole visual effect. It is also why the blocks are plain rectangles of constant depth
# rather than trapezoids: a constant perpendicular offset gives a clean parallel inner edge and
# still swallows every sheared column, which was checked rather than assumed.


# Overriding the contract, which is written for the facility's own box.

# --- what the weather works on, and what the paving is patterned with --------------------------
let colon_rustic  = stripes axis=y period=0.45 duty=0.22

# ================================================================================================
# THE TUSCAN ORDER — 6.30 m (7 D), drawn once at the origin, standing on y = 0
# ================================================================================================
#
# THE BASE — 0.45 m (0.5 D). A square plinth, one large torus and the fillet over it. That is the
# whole of a Tuscan base and the reason it is drawn as a section and revolved is the same reason
# _order.clip gives: a stacked torus is a drum.
#
#   0.000 - 0.180   plinth   0.4 M   square, half-width 0.63 (1.4 M)
#   0.180 - 0.360   torus    0.4 M   out to 0.585 (1.3 M)
#   0.360 - 0.450   fillet   0.2 M   at 0.495 (1.1 M), 0.045 proud of the shaft
let colon_base_core = box 0 0.18 -1   0.495 0.45 1
let colon_base_tor  = bead 0.495 0.18   0.585 0.36
let colon_base_sec  = union { colon_base_core colon_base_tor }
let colon_base_turn = revolve { colon_base_sec } axis=y
let colon_base_plin = box -0.63 0 -0.63   0.63 0.185 0.63 round=0.012
let colon_tus_base  = union { colon_base_plin colon_base_turn }

# THE SHAFT — 5.40 m (6 D), drawn from its own y = 0, unfluted, with entasis.
#
# 0.45 at the foot, 0.375 at the top (five-sixths, exactly), swelling 0.0375 over the straight
# taper at a third of the height. The arc through those three points has its centre at
# (-85.973, 1.5002) in the (radius, height) plane and a radius of 86.436. Those two are the
# CONSTRUCTION and not dimensions; the three that are dimensions are 0.450 = M, 0.375 = 5M/6 and
# 1.80 = 5.40/3.
#
# A Tuscan shaft is PLAIN. There are no flutes to leave a one-voxel fillet, and there is nothing
# here to widen or to protect: this order's share of the load is the count and the roundness, not
# thin matter.
let colon_shaft_arc = cylinder -85.973 1.5002 0  r=86.436 h=4 axis=z
let colon_shaft_bnd = box 0 0 -1   0.60 5.40 1
let colon_shaft_sec = intersection { colon_shaft_bnd colon_shaft_arc }
let colon_tus_shaft = revolve { colon_shaft_sec } axis=y

# THE CAPITAL — 0.45 m (0.5 D), drawn from its own y = 0.
#
#   0.000 - 0.045   astragal, the necking ring, out to 0.4275
#   0.045 - 0.090   neck, at the shaft's own 0.375
#   0.090 - 0.270   echinus, an ovolo out to 0.5175 (1.15 M)
#   0.270 - 0.315   fillet over it
#   0.315 - 0.450   abacus, square, half-width 0.5625 (1.25 M)
let colon_cap_core = box 0 0 -1   0.375 0.315 1
let colon_cap_astr = bead  0.375 0.000   0.4275 0.045
let colon_cap_ech  = ovolo 0.375 0.270   0.5175 0.090
let colon_cap_fil  = fillet 0 0.270   0.5175 0.315
let colon_cap_sec  = union { colon_cap_core colon_cap_astr colon_cap_ech colon_cap_fil }
let colon_cap_turn = revolve { colon_cap_sec } axis=y
let colon_cap_abac = box -0.5625 0.310 -0.5625   0.5625 0.45 0.5625 round=0.012
let colon_tus_cap  = union { colon_cap_turn colon_cap_abac }

# THE COLUMN, with a shoe: the plinth's own footprint sunk 0.05 into the stylobate and reaching
# 0.05 up inside the plinth, so the join is certain however far the grain moves either surface.
# One hundred and sixty-eight columns and one column 4 cm short of the ground is the fault that
# `components 1` exists to catch.
let colon_col_shoe = box -0.63 -0.05 -0.63   0.63 0.05 0.63 round=0.012
let colon_column = union {
    colon_tus_base
    translate { colon_tus_shaft } 0 0.45 0
    translate { colon_tus_cap } 0 5.85 0
    colon_col_shoe
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE BALUSTER — 0.675 m (1.5 M), and the URN — 1.34 m
# ================================================================================================
#
# The neck is 0.060 across: one voxel at metre 16, two at metre 32. There are 160 of them on the
# two arms and they are the thinnest stone in this building.
let colon_bal_rod  = box 0 0.00 -1   0.030 0.675 1
let colon_bal_low  = box 0 0.045 -1  0.036 0.135 1
let colon_bal_foot = bead 0.036 0.045   0.081 0.135
let colon_bal_vase = cyma_reversa 0.030 0.135   0.081 0.405
let colon_bal_ast  = bead 0.030 0.405   0.045 0.435
let colon_bal_cup  = cyma 0.030 0.435   0.063 0.585
let colon_bal_top  = fillet 0 0.585   0.072 0.615
let colon_bal_sec  = union { colon_bal_rod colon_bal_low colon_bal_foot colon_bal_vase
                             colon_bal_ast colon_bal_cup colon_bal_top }
let colon_bal_turn = revolve { colon_bal_sec } axis=y
let colon_bal_plin = box -0.09 0 -0.09   0.09 0.075 0.09 round=0.008
let colon_bal_cap  = box -0.081 0.60 -0.081   0.081 0.675 0.081 round=0.008
let colon_baluster = union { colon_bal_turn colon_bal_plin colon_bal_cap }

let colon_urn_stem  = box 0 0.135 -1   0.075 0.945 1
let colon_urn_foot  = cyma_reversa 0.075 0.135   0.165 0.225
let colon_urn_bwl_c = cylinder 0 0.585 0  r=0.27 h=2 axis=z
let colon_urn_bwl_b = box 0 0.27 -1   0.30 0.79 1
let colon_urn_bowl  = intersection { colon_urn_bwl_c colon_urn_bwl_b }
let colon_urn_lip   = cyma 0.135 0.79   0.24 0.90
let colon_urn_rim   = fillet 0 0.90   0.24 0.945
let colon_urn_sec   = union { colon_urn_stem colon_urn_foot colon_urn_bowl
                              colon_urn_lip colon_urn_rim }
let colon_urn_turn  = revolve { colon_urn_sec } axis=y
let colon_urn_plin  = box -0.18 0 -0.18   0.18 0.145 0.18 round=0.010
let colon_urn_lid   = intersection { ellipsoid 0 0.94 0 rx=0.20 ry=0.225 rz=0.20
                                     box -0.21 0.935 -0.21   0.21 1.16 0.21 }
let colon_urn_fin   = union { capsule 0 1.13 0   0 1.22 0 r=0.030
                              sphere 0 1.265 0 r=0.065 }
let colon_urn = union { colon_urn_plin colon_urn_turn colon_urn_lid colon_urn_fin }

# ================================================================================================
# THE FIGURES — four of them, 2.70 m, coarse on purpose
# ================================================================================================
#
# These are not sculpture and they are not trying to be. What they have to be is EIGHTY-FOUR
# separate irregular masses standing against the sky at a distance where each covers a handful of
# pixels, which is the one silhouette case nothing else on the estate produces. Each is nine or
# ten primitives round a common core; each stands on its own 0.45 x 0.54 plinth; each is turned to
# face the middle of the court where it is placed, and the four types are dealt round the twenty-
# one stations of an arm so that no two neighbours are the same shape.
let colon_fig_plin = box -0.225 0 -0.27   0.225 0.135 0.27 round=0.010
let colon_fig_robe = ellipsoid 0 0.90 0 rx=0.32 ry=0.78 rz=0.26
let colon_fig_hem  = ellipsoid 0 0.20 0 rx=0.34 ry=0.12 rz=0.29
let colon_fig_wast = ellipsoid 0 1.62 0 rx=0.25 ry=0.20 rz=0.20
let colon_fig_body = ellipsoid 0 2.00 0 rx=0.29 ry=0.32 rz=0.22
let colon_fig_shld = capsule -0.25 2.24 0   0.25 2.24 0 r=0.125
let colon_fig_neck = capsule 0 2.32 0   0 2.42 0 r=0.075
let colon_fig_head = sphere 0 2.545 0 r=0.145
let colon_fig_core = union { colon_fig_plin colon_fig_robe colon_fig_hem colon_fig_wast
                             colon_fig_body colon_fig_shld colon_fig_neck colon_fig_head }

# A: the right arm raised, the left gathering the robe
let colon_fig_a_up = capsule 0.24 2.24 0.02   0.42 2.66 0.16 r=0.090
let colon_fig_a_dn = capsule -0.24 2.20 0.02   -0.36 1.58 0.14 r=0.080
let colon_fig_a_fl = ellipsoid -0.30 1.10 0.10 rx=0.13 ry=0.55 rz=0.10
let colon_figure_a = union { colon_fig_core colon_fig_a_up colon_fig_a_dn colon_fig_a_fl }

# B: the left arm raised with a palm, the right across the body
let colon_fig_b_up = capsule -0.24 2.24 0.02   -0.40 2.62 0.10 r=0.090
let colon_fig_b_pl = capsule -0.42 2.60 0.10   -0.50 3.02 0.16 r=0.065
let colon_fig_b_ac = capsule 0.24 2.20 0.04   -0.02 1.86 0.22 r=0.078
let colon_fig_b_fl = ellipsoid 0.28 1.20 -0.10 rx=0.13 ry=0.60 rz=0.10
let colon_figure_b = union { colon_fig_core colon_fig_b_up colon_fig_b_pl
                             colon_fig_b_ac colon_fig_b_fl }

# C: cloaked, both arms down, a book held at the waist
let colon_fig_c_cl = ellipsoid 0 1.90 -0.14 rx=0.36 ry=0.62 rz=0.20
let colon_fig_c_la = capsule -0.24 2.18 0.04   -0.16 1.66 0.20 r=0.080
let colon_fig_c_ra = capsule 0.24 2.18 0.04   0.16 1.66 0.20 r=0.080
let colon_fig_c_bk = box -0.16 1.56 0.14   0.16 1.72 0.30 round=0.020
let colon_figure_c = union { colon_fig_core colon_fig_c_cl colon_fig_c_la
                             colon_fig_c_ra colon_fig_c_bk }

# D: a staff in the right hand, the left thrown out
let colon_fig_d_st = capsule 0.30 0.14 0.10   0.30 2.90 0.10 r=0.065
let colon_fig_d_ra = capsule 0.24 2.20 0.02   0.30 2.02 0.10 r=0.078
let colon_fig_d_la = capsule -0.24 2.22 0.02   -0.48 2.30 0.20 r=0.090
let colon_fig_d_fl = ellipsoid -0.34 1.40 0.14 rx=0.14 ry=0.72 rz=0.11
let colon_figure_d = union { colon_fig_core colon_fig_d_st colon_fig_d_ra
                             colon_fig_d_la colon_fig_d_fl }

# ================================================================================================
# THE UNITS — one bay of stylobate, entablature and balustrade, drawn once, straight
# ================================================================================================
#
# Local x runs ALONG the arm (2.40 m of it, so a 2.025 bay overlaps its neighbours by 0.1875 each
# side), local z runs OUT from the chord of the outer row, and local y is the world's. Every one
# of these is carried to twenty-six places by one rotation and one translation.

# THE STYLOBATE — three risers of 0.18 and three runs of 0.32, on both faces.
#
#   y -0.10 .. 0.18   bottom step   z -6.94 .. 1.54
#   y -0.10 .. 0.36   middle step   z -6.62 .. 1.22
#   y -0.10 .. 0.54   top step      z -6.30 .. 0.90
#
# It reaches 0.10 below the ground so that it certainly meets it, and 6.30 (14 M) in from the
# outer row's chord, which puts its inner edge 0.90 (2 M) inside the inner row where the ray is
# the normal and further where it is not.
let colon_sty_low = box -1.20 -0.10 -6.94   1.20 0.18 1.54 round=0.012
let colon_sty_mid = box -1.20 -0.10 -6.62   1.20 0.36 1.22 round=0.012
let colon_sty_top = box -1.20 -0.10 -6.30   1.20 0.54 0.90 round=0.012
let colon_sty_unit = union { colon_sty_low colon_sty_mid colon_sty_top }

# THE ENTABLATURE — 1.80 m (4 M), from 6.84 to 8.64, drawn from its own y = 0.
#
# A plain Doric-Tuscan section, divided in fortieths of its own height (0.045 = M/10):
#
#   0.000 - 0.270   first fascia          face at 0.360
#   0.270 - 0.450   second fascia         face at 0.450
#   0.450 - 0.540   taenia                face at 0.540, the plain projecting band
#   0.540 - 1.080   frieze                face at 0.450
#   1.080 - 1.170   bed mould, a cavetto  flaring 0.450 -> 0.630
#   1.170 - 1.305   MUTULE BAND           face at 0.630, with two mutules out to 0.900
#   1.305 - 1.620   corona                face at 0.945
#   1.620 - 1.800   crowning cyma         0.945 -> 1.080
#
# The mutules are the Doric ones: flat blocks 0.63 wide under the corona, one over each column and
# one over each intercolumniation, with THREE GUTTAE hanging 0.105 below each. A gutta is 0.090
# across — a voxel and a half at metre 16 — and there are 312 of them on the two arms. They are the finest
# matter in the stonework and they are meant to be at the edge of what survives.
let colon_ent_core = box -1.20 -0.050 -6.30   1.20 1.800 0.360 round=0.012
let colon_ent_fas2 = box -1.20 0.270 0.000   1.20 0.450 0.450 round=0.008
let colon_ent_taen = box -1.20 0.450 0.000   1.20 0.540 0.540 round=0.008
let colon_ent_frie = box -1.20 0.540 0.000   1.20 1.080 0.450
let colon_ent_bedc = box -1.20 1.080 0.000   1.20 1.170 0.460
let colon_ent_bed  = cavetto -1.20 1.080 0.450   1.20 1.170 0.630 run=x
let colon_ent_mutb = box -1.20 1.170 0.000   1.20 1.305 0.630 round=0.008
let colon_ent_coro = box -1.20 1.305 0.000   1.20 1.620 0.945 round=0.008
let colon_ent_crnb = box -1.20 1.620 0.000   1.20 1.800 0.950
let colon_ent_crn  = cyma -1.20 1.620 0.945   1.20 1.800 1.080 run=x

# One mutule and its three guttae, at local x = 0; the pair sits at +-0.50625 (half the bay), so
# one lands over a column and one over the gap between two.
let colon_ent_mut1 = box -0.315 1.170 0.600   0.315 1.305 0.900 round=0.008
let colon_ent_gut1 = cylinder 0 1.1400 0.750  r=0.045 h=0.15 axis=y
let colon_ent_gut3 = repeat { colon_ent_gut1 } x=0.180 nx=1
let colon_ent_mut  = union { colon_ent_mut1 colon_ent_gut3 }
let colon_ent_muts = union { translate { colon_ent_mut } -0.50625 0 0
                             translate { colon_ent_mut } 0.50625 0 0 }

# THE INNER FACE. It cannot be a run of the same section — the section faces out and the inside of
# a colonnade is a ceiling edge, not an elevation — so it gets a plain two-step lip: a corona and
# a crowning fillet, both running the same 2.40 m straight.
let colon_ent_icor = box -1.20 1.305 -6.57   1.20 1.620 -6.25 round=0.008
let colon_ent_icrn = box -1.20 1.620 -6.43   1.20 1.800 -6.25 round=0.008

let colon_ent_unit = union {
    colon_ent_core colon_ent_fas2 colon_ent_taen colon_ent_frie
    colon_ent_bedc colon_ent_bed colon_ent_mutb colon_ent_muts
    colon_ent_coro colon_ent_crnb colon_ent_crn
    colon_ent_icor colon_ent_icrn
}

# THE COFFERS. Three sunken panels in the soffit of every bay, 1.35 (3 M) square and 0.225 (M/2)
# deep, on a pitch of 1.80 m through the depth: three to a bay, seventy-eight per arm. A coffer is the textbook soft-
# occlusion gradient and these are in a ceiling that has no direct light on it at all.
#
# THEY ARE CUT FROM THE WHOLE RING AND NOT FROM ONE BLOCK. Adjacent blocks overlap by 0.375 m at
# the outer face and by metres at the inner one, so a coffer cut out of its own block alone is
# simply filled in again by its neighbour — sixty-six recesses that are not there, and nothing in
# the numbers would have said so.
let colon_ent_cof1 = box -0.675 -0.10 -5.175   0.675 0.175 -3.825 round=0.020
let colon_ent_cof2 = box -0.675 -0.10 -3.375   0.675 0.175 -2.025 round=0.020
let colon_ent_cof3 = box -0.675 -0.10 -1.575   0.675 0.175 -0.225 round=0.020
let colon_ent_cofs = union { colon_ent_cof1 colon_ent_cof2 colon_ent_cof3 }

# THE BALUSTRADE — 1.08 m (2.4 M), from 8.64 to 9.72, drawn from its own y = 0.
#
#   0.000 - 0.270   kerb, the full run
#   0.270 - 0.945   the balusters, 0.675 (1.5 M) of them
#   0.945 - 1.080   rail, the full run
#
# One station pier 0.36 wide at the +x end of the unit (which is the end nearer station i, since
# the run is taken backwards -- see the frame note above), one mid-bay pier 0.27 wide in the
# middle, and four balusters between them at 0.3375 and 0.675 either side of the middle. The
# narrowest gap in it is 0.0675 — one voxel at metre 16.
#
# The URN stands on the mid-bay pier and is part of this unit, so there are forty of them per two
# arms and they cost one line. The STATUE stands on the station pier and is placed separately,
# because each one is turned to face the court and they are dealt round four types.
let colon_blu_kerb = box -1.20 -0.050 0.420   1.20 0.270 1.110 round=0.010
let colon_blu_rail = box -1.20 0.945 0.420   1.20 1.080 1.110 round=0.010
let colon_blu_pier = box 0.8325 -0.050 0.390   1.1925 1.080 1.140 round=0.012
let colon_blu_mid  = box -0.135 -0.050 0.390   0.135 1.080 1.140 round=0.012
let colon_blu_b1   = translate { colon_baluster } -0.675 0.255 0.765
let colon_blu_b2   = translate { colon_baluster } -0.3375 0.255 0.765
let colon_blu_b3   = translate { colon_baluster } 0.3375 0.255 0.765
let colon_blu_b4   = translate { colon_baluster } 0.675 0.255 0.765
let colon_blu_urn  = translate { colon_urn } 0 1.030 0.765
let colon_blu_unit = union {
    colon_blu_kerb colon_blu_rail colon_blu_pier colon_blu_mid
    colon_blu_b1 colon_blu_b2 colon_blu_b3 colon_blu_b4
}
let colon_urn_unit = colon_blu_urn

# The terminal pier: the last station of an arm has no bay beyond it to carry its pier, so it gets
# a short one of its own, 0.60 of run, lapping 0.49 into the last bay unit.
let colon_blu_ekerb = box -0.30 -0.050 0.420   0.30 0.270 1.110 round=0.010
let colon_blu_erail = box -0.30 0.945 0.420   0.30 1.080 1.110 round=0.010
let colon_blu_epier = box -0.18 -0.050 0.390   0.18 1.080 1.140 round=0.012
let colon_blu_end = union { colon_blu_ekerb colon_blu_erail colon_blu_epier }

# ================================================================================================
# THE EAST ARM — twenty-six placements of each unit, on the chords of the outer row
# ================================================================================================
#
# Twenty bays, and three end caps at each end slid 0.90 apart along the run. The caps are there
# because THE ROWS SHEAR: on the terminal ray the inner three columns stand up to 2.3 m past the
# last bay's midpoint, measured along the chord, and without the caps three columns at each end of
# each arm would have half a plinth on nothing. That was found by checking all 168 of them rather
# than by looking at a picture.
#
# The caps then jut past the end ray, so the whole arm is cut back to a clean RADIAL end face
# 1.35 m (3 M) beyond it. Two half-spaces do that, and the same two are taken off the stylobate
# and the entablature. Every column's full 0.63 m plinth survives the cut — checked, all 168.
let colon_cut_n = plane 0.7071068 0 -0.7071068 at=-1.3500000
let colon_cut_s = plane 0.8424577 0 0.5387625 at=-1.3500000

let colon_sty_00 = translate { rotate { colon_sty_unit } y=0.4602786 } 6.9760 0.0000 -13.8823
let colon_sty_01 = translate { rotate { colon_sty_unit } y=0.4602786 } 7.8481 0.0000 -13.6600
